<GuideLayout slug="watch-cctv-on-samsung-tv">
    <p>
        A big TV is a good screen for security cameras: someone at the door,
        the shop counter, the pet or the baby, all without picking up a phone.
        There are four practical ways to get camera feeds onto a Samsung Smart
        TV, and each has trade-offs.
    </p>

    <h2>1. HDMI from the recorder</h2>
    <p>
        Most NVRs and DVRs have an HDMI output. Run a cable to a TV and the
        recorder's own interface appears on the screen. It is simple and
        reliable, and it also gives playback of recordings. The drawbacks are
        the cable run and that the TV becomes dedicated to the recorder.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Cast or mirror from a phone</h2>
    <p>
        Open the camera maker's phone app and cast or screen-mirror it to the
        TV. It needs no extra hardware, but it ties up your phone, can be
        laggy, and the phone has to stay on and nearby.
    </p>

    <h2>3. The camera brand's TV app</h2>
    <p>
        Some brands publish a Smart TV app. Check the Samsung TV app store for
        your camera's brand. Support varies a lot by brand and model, and
        many brands have no Samsung TV app.
    </p>

    <h2>4. An RTSP or ONVIF viewer app on the TV</h2>
    <p>
        Cameras and recorders that offer RTSP or ONVIF can be shown by a
        generic viewer app, whatever the brand. The TV connects over your home
        or office network straight to the camera. This works across brands, but
        the TV and cameras must be on the same local network, and you need the
        device's address and login. Our guide on
        <a href="/guides/find-rtsp-url-cctv-camera/">finding your camera's RTSP URL</a>
        explains how.
    </p>

    <h2>Which should you choose?</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Want playback and the most reliable picture:</strong> HDMI from the recorder.</li>
        <li><strong>Need a quick look occasionally:</strong> cast from your phone.</li>
        <li><strong>Mixed brands, no cables, live viewing on an existing TV:</strong> an RTSP/ONVIF viewer app.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Limits to know about</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Live video can arrive a few seconds behind real time, so none of these replaces an alarm system.</li>
        <li>Cloud-only Wi-Fi cameras without RTSP or ONVIF will not work with a viewer app.</li>
        <li>Use the lower-resolution stream if your network is slow, and the main stream when you want detail.</li>
        <li>Make sure you have the legal right to view any camera you connect.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>GroverHQ Camera Viewer</h2>
    <p>
        <a href={CAMERA_VIEWER.path}>GroverHQ Camera Viewer</a> is a live-view
        app for Samsung Smart TVs in India that finds cameras on your network
        or lets you add one by IP address, and works with IP cameras and NVR/DVR
        channels that offer RTSP or ONVIF. It is live view only: no recording
        and no playback. The free version shows standard definition with a
        small watermark, and an optional {CAMERA_VIEWER.priceText} unlock
        removes the watermark and adds HD. {
            CAMERA_VIEWER.storeLive
                ? "It is available from the Samsung TV Store."
                : "It is coming soon to the Samsung TV Store."
        }
    </p>

